$ weave --help
Terminal client for ACP agents

Usage: weave [OPTIONS] [-- <COMMAND>...]
       weave <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run         Run one prompt in a headless thread in the weave Host, printing the reply
  threads     Threads open in the weave Host: list, read, prompt or steer them, stop a turn, or close one
  agents      The agents weave launches: list them, move them to their newest releases, sign in to or out of one, or try one with a prompt
  schedules   The Dispatcher's schedules, which start a headless turn on one of your Hosts, or prompt one of your Bots, at the times a cron expression gives or once: list, add, remove, pause, resume or run one, or see what they did
  hosts       Your Hosts the Dispatcher starts work on: list them, have one join it, or remove one
  projects    Your Projects, which the Dispatcher keeps, each with a location on one or more of your Hosts: list them, register a directory as one, rename one, or remove one
  bots        Your Bots, which the Dispatcher keeps: assistants with a home of their own on one of your Hosts, working there in one thread at a time. List them, add, show, change or remove one, send one a prompt, or start its new thread
  pair        Pair one of your devices, or another computer, with the Dispatcher, through which it reaches your Hosts. Run it where the Dispatcher runs
  devices     Devices paired with the Dispatcher: list them, or revoke one
  terminals   The weave Host's terminals: show one here, as the TUI does in a Rex pane
  worktrees   Git worktrees: what worktrunk asks the weave Host before it removes one
  daemon      Manage the weave Host, which runs agents so their threads outlive the TUI
  dispatcher  The Dispatcher, which starts work on your Hosts while you're away: run, start, stop or install it, or pair this computer with it where it runs on another
  help        Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [COMMAND]...
          A custom agent command and its arguments, given after `--`

Options:
      --agent <AGENT>
          Agent to launch: a preset (claude, codex, gemini), one defined in the config file, or an ACP registry agent's id (`weave agents` lists them)

      --trace [<TRACE>]
          Append a protocol trace of the agent to this file, viewable with agent-client-protocol-trace-viewer. Through the weave Host, which writes the trace in its own directory and says where, give it without a file

      --config <CONFIG>
          Config file. Defaults to ~/.config/weave/config.toml

      --cwd <CWD>
          Thread working directory. Defaults to the current directory

      --add-dir <DIR>
          An extra workspace root for the thread; repeatable. Needs agent support

      --no-fs
          Don't offer the agent fs/read_text_file and fs/write_text_file

      --no-terminal
          Don't offer the agent terminals for running commands

      --resume [<THREAD_ID>]
          Reopen a thread: by id, or (in the TUI) choose one from a list when no id is given

      --continue
          Reopen the thread last used in this directory (one open in the daemon first), with the agent last used here unless --agent or `--` names one

      --log-file <LOG_FILE>
          Write diagnostics to this file (with --no-daemon, agent stderr too)

      --no-alt-screen
          Run inline instead of fullscreen: a viewport below the prompt, with history in the terminal's own scrollback. Also `alternate_screen = "never"` under `[tui]` in the config

      --no-daemon
          Run the agent in a Host inside this process rather than the shared weave Host, so its threads end when weave does. Also `enabled = false` under `[daemon]` in the config

      --host <HOST>
          Open the thread on another of your Hosts, by its id or name as `weave hosts` lists them, through the Dispatcher. A new thread works in this directory's counterpart under the home there, unless --cwd names one there

      --project <PROJECT>
          Work in a Project, by its name or id as `weave projects` lists them: in its location on the Host, a repository's primary worktree. On this computer's Host unless --host names another; a Project with no location there is refused

  -h, --help
          Print help

  -V, --version
          Print version

$ weave run --help
Run one prompt in a headless thread in the weave Host, printing the reply

Usage: weave run [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]... [-- <COMMAND>...]

Arguments:
  [PROMPT]...
          The prompt; read from stdin when omitted or `-`. Before the agent's arguments, whose custom command comes last, after `--`

  [COMMAND]...
          A custom agent command and its arguments, given after `--`

Options:
      --agent <AGENT>
          Agent to launch: a preset (claude, codex, gemini), one defined in the config file, or an ACP registry agent's id (`weave agents` lists them)

      --trace [<TRACE>]
          Append a protocol trace of the agent to this file, viewable with agent-client-protocol-trace-viewer. Through the weave Host, which writes the trace in its own directory and says where, give it without a file

      --config <CONFIG>
          Config file. Defaults to ~/.config/weave/config.toml

      --cwd <CWD>
          Thread working directory. Defaults to the current directory

      --add-dir <DIR>
          An extra workspace root for the thread; repeatable. Needs agent support

      --no-fs
          Don't offer the agent fs/read_text_file and fs/write_text_file

      --no-terminal
          Don't offer the agent terminals for running commands

      --resume [<THREAD_ID>]
          Reopen a thread: by id, or (in the TUI) choose one from a list when no id is given

      --continue
          Reopen the thread last used in this directory (one open in the daemon first), with the agent last used here unless --agent or `--` names one

      --approve <KINDS>
          Tool kinds to allow, comma-separated: read, edit, delete, move, search, execute, think, fetch, switch_mode, other; or all. Everything else is rejected

      --mode <MODE>
          The mode a new thread starts in, by the agent's id or name for it, such as Claude's `default`; the agent's configured `mode` when omitted, which approves for itself what `--approve` doesn't

      --detach
          Leave the turn running in the weave Host and print the thread's id

      --key <KEY>
          A name of your choosing, such as a UUID: running again with it, after a dropped connection, starts nothing twice and follows the turn the first run started

      --label <LABEL>
          A label for a new thread, to find it by (`weave threads list --label`); repeatable

      --timeout <DURATION>
          Stop waiting after this long, exiting 8; the turn goes on. Seconds, or with a unit: 500ms, 90s, 5m, 2h

      --keep-waiting
          Wait on while the turn waits on a person to answer it, rather than exiting 7

      --json
          Print the thread's journal events (`thread/event`) as JSON lines instead of the reply's text; with --detach, the Host's answer (`thread/run`)

      --no-daemon
          Run the agent in this process rather than the weave Host

      --host <HOST>
          Run it on another of your Hosts, by its id or name as `weave hosts` lists them, through the Dispatcher. A new thread works in this directory's counterpart under the home there, unless --cwd names one there

      --project <PROJECT>
          Run it in a Project, by its name or id as `weave projects` lists them: in its location on the Host, a repository's primary worktree. On this computer's Host unless --host names another; a Project with no location there is refused

  -h, --help
          Print help

$ weave threads --help
Threads open in the weave Host: list, read, prompt or steer them, stop a turn, or close one

Usage: weave threads [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  list           List the threads open in the weave Host (the default)
  read           Read a thread: how it lists and its latest turn on stderr, and the agent's last message in that turn on stdout
  prompt         Send a thread a prompt: it starts a turn at once if none runs, or waits in the thread's queue for the running one to end. The prompt's id is its turn's
  steer          Send a message into a thread's running turn, without starting another; it fails when no turn runs
  cancel         Stop the turn a thread is running, as Esc does in the TUI
  close          Close a thread for every client: its turn ends and its agent stops. `weave --resume` can still reopen it from the agent's history
  watch          Print the Host's threads as they open, change and close, as JSON lines: a `threads/updated` for each at first, then one for every change, until stopped
  follow         Follow an open thread as it goes, without being one of its clients: the agent's reply as text, or with --json its journal events (`thread/event`) as JSON lines
  set-successor  Name the thread an open thread's work goes on in, its successor: clients following it may follow that, and reports to it go there. Named once
  label          Add labels to an open thread and take others off, and print its labels. Labels mean nothing to the Host: they're for finding threads, and labelled threads are kept past the Host's two weeks
  help           Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --host <HOST>
          The threads of another of your Hosts, by its id or name as `weave hosts` lists them, through the Dispatcher

      --project <PROJECT>
          List only the threads in this Project, as `weave threads list --project` does

  -h, --help
          Print help

$ weave threads list --help
List the threads open in the weave Host (the default)

Usage: weave threads list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --activity <ACTIVITY>
          Only the threads doing this
          
          [possible values: idle, running, waiting]

      --label <LABEL>
          Only the threads that carry this label; given again, those that carry every one

      --project <PROJECT>
          Only the threads in this Project, by its name or id as `weave projects` lists them: in its location on the Host, a repository's worktrees included, and not in a Project inside it

      --json
          Print them as JSON

      --host <HOST>
          The threads of another of your Hosts, by its id or name as `weave hosts` lists them, through the Dispatcher

  -h, --help
          Print help

