$ weave bots --help
Your Bots, which the Dispatcher keeps: assistants with a home of their own on one of your Hosts, working there in one thread at a time. List them, add, show, change or remove one, send one a prompt, or start its new thread

Usage: weave bots [COMMAND]

Commands:
  list        The Bots, what each is doing, and its thread (the default)
  add         Add a Bot on one of your Hosts, which makes its home first. It has no thread until the first prompt you send it
  show        A Bot: how it's set up, its threads and its schedules
  update      Change a Bot. A thread it has open keeps the approvals and mode it started with
  remove      Forget a Bot and its schedules. Its threads and its home stay
  send        Send a Bot a prompt: into its thread, or starting its first
  new-thread  Start a Bot's new thread: its thread saves what it knows to its home first
  help        Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help

$ weave bots list --help
The Bots, what each is doing, and its thread (the default)

Usage: weave bots list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --json
          Print them as JSON

  -h, --help
          Print help

$ weave bots add --help
Add a Bot on one of your Hosts, which makes its home first. It has no thread until the first prompt you send it

Usage: weave bots add [OPTIONS] --agent <AGENT> <NAME>

Arguments:
  <NAME>
          Its name, unique among your Bots ignoring case

Options:
      --agent <AGENT>
          The agent its threads run, by its id on the Bot's Host, as `weave agents` lists them there

      --host <HOST>
          The Host its threads run on, by its id or name as `weave hosts` lists them; this computer's when omitted, which has to have joined the Dispatcher

      --home <DIR>
          Its home on that Host: the git repository of its own its threads run in and its memory is kept in, whole or under ~ there; on this computer's Host, from this directory too. ~/bots/<name> when omitted. The Host makes it, with its first memory files, unless one of them is there

      --job <TEXT>
          What it's for, in your words, which its first AGENTS.md says

      --approve <KINDS>
          Tool kinds its threads approve without asking you, comma-separated: read, edit, delete, move, search, execute, think, fetch, switch_mode, other; or all. It asks you about the rest

      --mode <MODE>
          The mode its threads start in; the agent's configured one when omitted

      --new-thread-at <HH:MM>
          Start a new thread every day at this time, in --zone, once its thread has had a turn

      --zone <ZONE>
          The IANA time zone its times are in, such as Africa/Johannesburg; the Dispatcher's computer's when omitted

      --pause-after <DURATION>
          How long a request in its thread may wait for you before its schedules pause, until it's answered: 90s, 30m, 12h. 12h when omitted

      --json
          Print the Bot as JSON

  -h, --help
          Print help

$ weave bots show --help
A Bot: how it's set up, its threads and its schedules

Usage: weave bots show [OPTIONS] <BOT>

Arguments:
  <BOT>
          The Bot's name or id

Options:
